<ToolPage title="OSHA incidence rate calculator: TRIR and DART rate" description="Work out your total recordable incidence rate (TRIR), DART rate and days-away rate from the OSHA 300 log and hours worked, with 2024 averages to compare." h1="TRIR and DART" lead="TRIR, DART and days-away rates from your OSHA 300 log." crumb="OSHA incidence rate" path="/osha-incidence-rate-calculator/">
  <Calc slot="calculator" tool="osha" />
  <h2 id="how">How the numbers are worked out</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rate</strong> = cases × 200,000 ÷ hours worked by all employees in the year. The 200,000 hours are 100 full-time workers at 40 hours a week for 50 weeks, so every rate is per 100 full-time workers.</li>
    <li><strong>TRIR</strong> counts the non-fatal recordable cases, columns H, I and J of the OSHA 300 log, as the BLS calculator does; if there were deaths (column G), the rate counting them too is shown as well, since many prequalification forms ask for it. <strong>DART</strong> counts cases with days away, restricted work or a job transfer, columns H and I; the days-away rate counts column H.</li>
    <li>In 2024, private industry averaged 2.3 total recordable cases and 1.4 DART cases per 100 full-time workers; construction 2.2 and 1.3. Hours worked leave out paid time off such as vacation and sick leave.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, <a href="https://www.bls.gov/iif/overview/compute-nonfatal-incidence-rates.htm">How To Compute Your Firm's Incidence Rate for Safety Management</a>: the formula and its worked example (read 2026-11-11).</li>
    <li>U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, <a href="https://www.bls.gov/web/osh/table-1-industry-rates-national.htm">Table 1. Incidence rates of nonfatal occupational injuries and illnesses by industry and case types, 2024</a> (published 22 January 2026).</li>
  </ul>
</ToolPage>
